{"parentUuid":"8780a5cb-320b-4178-92e1-7518152f43e4","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace","sessionId":"cc8c6da4-227b-4b6f-9ed6-09f963e4c9fc","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-frolicking-rainbow","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_016RHqThCKtzgDrRXYvjqrg1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There are a couple of duplicate jobIds. This is because the `reduce` operator in a streaming context emits intermediate results. The `KeyedCoProcessFunction` can emit multiple times for the same job when both sides arrive. Let me reconsider the approach.\n\nThe issue is that the streaming `reduce` emits every time it gets a new element. So `maxStageCounts` will emit multiple times per job as each session window fires. Then the co-process function may also emit multiple times.\n\nFor bounded data, the final output of a `reduce` will be the correct value, but `writeAsText` will capture all intermediate results. I need a different approach.\n\nLet me rethink this. Since the data is bounded (from collections), I should use a more batch-oriented approach. The cleanest solution would be:\n\n1. Use session windows to count tasks per stage\n2. Use a `ProcessFunction` with state to track both max count and job-finished status, accumulating everything, and only emit on close/finish using a timer or end-of-stream\n\nActually, the simplest correct approach for bounded data: use `windowAll` or `process` functions that buffer and emit at the end. But a better approach is to avoid the reduce intermediate emission problem.\n\nLet me use a `KeyedProcessFunction` that accumulates all stage counts and job-finished signals, and uses an event-time timer at max watermark to emit the final result.\n\nWait — actually the simplest fix is: since both data sources are bounded collections, Flink will process all records, and then watermarks will advance to Long.MAX_VALUE. The `reduce` will emit intermediate resu